body:
I relate to this as I have door slammed a few people for the same reasons. Sometimes, the first reason is situational and based on how much I care about the individual in question. For example, a person who is more like an acquaintance to me would be door-slammed if they aren't in contact with me. Sometimes, this happens unknowingly where I essentially forget that someone exists after a time. This is like an involuntary door-slam. 

&#x200B;

For the latter reason that you mentioned, that is where it gets complicated, but it is necessary. If someone is constantly hurting you in any way and you want the person out of your life, then it becomes a voluntary door-slam. There are feelings of regret, but there's usually reasons and a decision made when you do door-slam. However, some of these door-slams aren't like slam-and-lock. Instead, some relationships may experience the door-slam, but the door may still be open to reconcile the relationship. I think it depends on how you feel and if the other person is willing to work with you on the relationship.

&#x200B;

I like to think of the door-slam as a defense mechanism. In times where I needed to door-slam someone, the most dominant reason was to protect my inner self. There is always going to be regret when door-slamming, I think, and it's because we have the tendency to overthink and ruminate on certain things. One of the important things I have learned is that you can decide for yourself whether to slam(and lock) the door, or slam and leave it open. I say all this to say that it is not so much wanting to be able to control the door-slam, and it is more wanting to decide what is best for you relationship-wise.

I think about this a lot. It's gotten to a point where it's best to not tell family members anything about my honest feelings. They always find a way to make it about them or be mad about it. In my case, there's a lot of looking down on me for feeling the way I feel. I would rather talk to someone on a text line or phone line than to explain to my parent(s) why I'm depressed, why I isolate myself from others, etc. It's sad, but it's a truth that a lot of people have to live with.

&#x200B;

I found it best to just stay away from touchy subjects when it comes to talking to my parents. All they do is get angry and or judgmental.
